  "grid_title": "WHERE HEADACHES HURT",
  "why": "Tension-type: the most common kind; a pressing or tightening band on both sides, not worse with activity. Migraine: often one-sided and throbbing, worse with movement, with nausea or sensitivity to light and sound; attacks usually last 4 to 72 hours. Aura: about 1 in 4 people with migraine get visual (or other) symptoms like zigzag or flashing lights. Cluster: severe pain around one eye or temple, with a watery eye or runny nose on that side, in bouts lasting weeks or months. \"Sinus\" headache: in a study of almost 3,000 people with repeated self- or doctor-diagnosed sinus headache and no infection, 80% met full migraine criteria. Rebound (medication-overuse) headache: using painkillers for headaches on most days of the month (10-15 or more) for months can make headaches more frequent. Thunderclap: sudden, severe pain that peaks in under a minute is an emergency: call 911. Headaches that keep coming back: see a doctor.",
